o the line stops at another colored square. But vfrontier covers whole column and underfill only fills background cells - so it naturally stops at non-bg cells. Good. But if a column has two colored cells, the region between them also gets filled? underfill fills background cells in the column, so yes cells between two colored cells in same column get filled with 8. Hmm, that would fill between. Let me check: column with colored at rows 0 and 2, background at row 1. vfrontier includes all rows, underfill fills row1 with 8. So yes filled between. The rule says lines go until reaching another colored square - so between them filled? It's ambiguous but verifier does that.\n\nI'll demonstrate mostly simple cases but also include a case with two colored cells in a column to show full fill.\n\nActually to best teach the rule I should show variety. Let me make examples:\n\nA) 2x2 simple with one color.\nB) 3x3 with one cell color.\nC) 2x3 with colored in one column.\nD) 3x3 with two colors in different columns.\nE) a case with a full row of color.\n\nBut careful: the tiling makes output 2x scaled. The student must infer tiling and column filling.\n\nLet me pick inputs with distinct colors so palette matters (mostcolor = background 0).\n\nLet me choose:\n\nExample A: 3x3\n[[0,2,0],\n [0,0,0],\n [0,0,0]]\nfg at (0,1) color2. column1 filled: rows1,2 become 8. Grid [[0,2,0],[0,8,0],[0,8,0]]. tile -> 6x6.\n\nExample B: 2x3\n[[3,0,0],\n [0,0,0]]\nfg at (0,0). column0 filled row1 -> [[3,0,0],[8,0,0]]. tile 4x6.\n\nExample C: 3x3 with two colors same? Let me do different columns:\n[[0,4,0],\n [0,0,0],\n [0,0,7]]\nfg at (0,1) and (2,2). columns 1 and 2 filled. Grid:\n[[0,4,8],\n [0,8,8],\n [0,8,7]]. tile.\n\nExample D: vertical two same color column:\n[[5,0,0],\n [0,0,0],\n [5,0,0]]\nfg column0 both cells; fill row1 col0 with 8. Grid [[5,0,0],[8,0,0],[5,0,0]]. tile.\n\nThese show variety. Good.\n\nAlso maybe an example showing a column with no fg stays all background (columns 1,2 i